 (Rule 3) | The campaign's go/no-go and cleared-model list determine the productization epic's scope (\"for the models the validation campaign clears\"). True gate with `rewrite` impact: a partial clear rewrites the model list; a full no-go removes the epic. Modeled as a rewrite gate rather than conditional branch files because the outcome space is the set of subsets of the supported model set (not a bounded ≤3 enumeration). |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must state the validated model list and the productized runtime's support surface, so it cannot be authored before productization completes. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Training documentation reflects the delivered PyTorch ROCm support. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers Kueue quota usage for amd.com/gpu. |\n| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 | Rules 14/15 | The campaign validates vLLM serving only (HLR-1). Training support and Kueue quota work do not build on the campaign's outcome — they proceed in parallel; hardware contention is a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n| E002 ∥ E003, E002 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 | Different repos/components (Serving Runtimes vs Training Operator/Distributed Workloads), no shared artifacts. |\n| E003 ∥ E004 | Rules 1/15 | Different components (Training Operator vs Distributed Workloads) even though the same team owns both. The training epic's Kueue-admission verification exercises the quota configuration as an AC gate (configuration reference, Rule 13 exception), not a DAG edge. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 |\n\nFull coverage; no orphaned HLRs. HLR-3 splits into E003/E004 on a component\nboundary (Training Operator vs Distributed Workloads), not a priority split.\nHLR-4 (P2) maps to E005 at P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n(strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1).\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- All epic `component` values are canonical names from\n  `.context/rhai-components.txt`, matched verbatim to the strategy's Affected\n  Components.\n- **KServe is passive** (no code changes indicated; the strategy lists it as\n  affected without a change description). Per Step 2, validation of serving on\n  AMD nodes under KServe InferenceServices becomes acceptance criteria on the\n  nearest active component's epic (E002, same team RHOAI Serving) — no separate\n  KServe epic.\n- **E005 component**: the canonical list has `Documentation`; the strategy does\n  not name an owning team for it (Impacted Teams table missing). Assigned to\n  RHOAI Serving as the team owning the P0 serving scope; flagged in Health\n  Warnings.\n- All four strategy components exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n  (rhoai-3.0: kserve.md, serving-runtimes.md, training-operator.md,\n  distributed-workloads.md) — no new-component onboarding chains and no\n  konflux-onboarding epics are needed; all are already Konflux-onboarded.\n\n## Investigation Analysis\n\nUnknowns scanned from Open Questions, risks, and assumptions:\n\n1. **Hardware access** (Open Question 1): procurement/partner-lab access for\n   dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is unresolved. The answer does not change which\n   epics exist or what they do — the campaign happens either way — so this is an\n   availability precondition, not an Investigation. It is recorded as a risk on\n   E001; securing access is part of E001's work, but access being granted is not\n   an acceptance criterion (externally controlled).\n2. **Upstream ROCm build cadence** (Open Question 2): whether AMD's runtime\n   image releases align with release trains. This affects the image-sourcing\n   approach inside E002 (consume AMD images vs self-build), not the existence or\n   boundaries of any epic. Recorded by the campaign (which images it used) and\n   handled as an AC + risk on E002.\n3. **Parity outcomes per model** (HLR-1): the go/no-go and cleared-model list\n   genuinely change what E002 does → Investigation epic E001 with a `rewrite`\n   gate on E002.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (AMD hardware access and AMD-controlled ROCm image\ncadence) has uncertain timing; partial value is deliverable without it:\n\n- **Tier 1** (independent, starts immediately, parallel): E001 campaign\n  (risk-reduction validation), E003 training support, E004 Kueue quota\n  handling.\n- **Tier 2** (gated): E002 serving runtime productization, gated by the E001\n  go/no-go.\n- E005 documentation completes after all implementations (Rule 11).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from the architecture-context\n  owning teams (RHOAI Serving: KServe, Serving Runtimes; Distributed Workloads:\n  Training Operator, Distributed Workloads). Epic-level ACs were derived from\n  HLR wording.\n- **Infrastructure not in platform inventory**: HLR-3 assumes `amd.com/gpu`\n  resources on nodes; the component that exposes them (AMD GPU operator / ROCm\n  device plugin stack) is not in the platform inventory — the architecture\n  context lists only the NVIDIA GPU Operator, as an external dependency rather\n  than a platform component. The validation campaign (E001) includes standing\n  up and validating this stack on the campaign nodes (Rule 22 validation). If\n  the go/no-go clears, platform-level support for the AMD GPU operator on\n  customer clusters is not covered by the strategy's Affected Components and\n  needs a follow-up scope decision.\n- **Docs team assumption**: E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving by assumption\n  (Documentation has no architecture-context file and the strategy names no\n  docs owner). Confirm the owning team before scheduling.\n- **E001 size**: E001 is estimated at ~2-3 sprints (cluster stack bring-up plus\n  per-model functional and performance runs across the supported model set);\n  kept whole because the go/no-go requires the full model-set picture from one\n  consistent hardware/stack configuration, and a split would fragment the parity\n  dataset across different node states. Re-split trigger: if the supported model\n  set exceeds ~8 models or stack bring-up consumes more than one sprint, split\n  the per-model-group parity runs into follow-on validation epics that reuse\n  the stood-up cluster configuration.\n- **No priority inversions detected**: E001 is P0 both by HLR-1 and by blocking\n  the P0 productization epic; E003/E004 are P1 matching HLR-3; E005 is P1 by the\n  docs-authoring exception (HLR-4 itself is P2 — the epic priority is derived\n  from the strategy's overall `Major` priority, not pushed onto upstream epics).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32997,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a07a6fa63d415b0f","agent_id":"ab43bec2ef90fc9be","session_id":"1580befd-2500-423d-8dd5-ee8176ac89f3","uuid":"f7411177-12d8-4f18-943a-cade413de807","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:24:37.074Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020 into epics"}
