{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf9438fe55d2c226","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n10\t\n11\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training.\n12\t\n13\t## Triage\n14\t\n15\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans four components (KServe, Serving\n16\tRuntimes, Training Operator, Distributed Workloads), two owning teams, and HLRs\n17\tat three priority levels, with a hardware-gated validation campaign determining\n18\tproductization scope. Not docs-only: three of four HLRs require code and\n19\tconfiguration work.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM serving functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support in Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for AMD GPU workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n30\t\n31\t## Dependency DAG\n32\t\n33\t```mermaid\n34\tgraph TD\n35\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 ROCm serving runtime]\n36\t    E002 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Documentation]\n37\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 PyTorch ROCm training] --> E005\n38\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E002 | Investigation edge (Rule 3) | The campaign's go/no-go and cleared-model list determine the productization epic's scope (\"for the models the validation campaign clears\"). True gate with `rewrite` impact: a partial clear rewrites the model list; a full no-go removes the epic. Modeled as a rewrite gate rather than conditional branch files because the outcome space is the set of subsets of the supported model set (not a bounded ≤3 enumeration). |\n46\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must state the validated model list and the productized runtime's support surface, so it cannot be authored before productization completes. |\n47\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Training documentation reflects the delivered PyTorch ROCm support. |\n48\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers Kueue quota usage for amd.com/gpu. |\n49\t| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 | Rules 14/15 | The campaign validates vLLM serving only (HLR-1). Training support and Kueue quota work do not build on the campaign's outcome — they proceed in parallel; hardware contention is a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n50\t| E002 ∥ E003, E002 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 | Different repos/components (Serving Runtimes vs Training Operator/Distributed Workloads), no shared artifacts. |\n51\t| E003 ∥ E004 | Rules 1/15 | Different components (Training Operator vs Distributed Workloads) even though the same team owns both. The training epic's Kueue-admission verification exercises the quota configuration as an AC gate (configuration reference, Rule 13 exception), not a DAG edge. |\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n56\t|-----|----------|---------|\n57\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n58\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 |\n59\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 |\n60\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 |\n61\t\n62\tFull coverage; no orphaned HLRs. HLR-3 splits into E003/E004 on a component\n63\tboundary (Training Operator vs Distributed Workloads), not a priority split.\n64\tHLR-4 (P2) maps to E005 at P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n65\t(strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1).\n66\t\n67\t## Component Mapping Notes\n68\t\n69\t- All epic `component` values are canonical names from\n70\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`, matched verbatim to the strategy's Affected\n71\t  Components.\n72\t- **KServe is passive** (no code changes indicated; the strategy lists it as\n73\t  affected without a change description). Per Step 2, validation of serving on\n74\t  AMD nodes under KServe InferenceServices becomes acceptance criteria on the\n75\t  nearest active component's epic (E002, same team RHOAI Serving) — no separate\n76\t  KServe epic.\n77\t- **E005 component**: the canonical list has `Documentation`; the strategy does\n78\t  not name an owning team for it (Impacted Teams table missing). Assigned to\n79\t  RHOAI Serving as the team owning the P0 serving scope; flagged in Health\n80\t  Warnings.\n81\t- All four strategy components exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n82\t  (rhoai-3.0: kserve.md, serving-runtimes.md, training-operator.md,\n83\t  distributed-workloads.md) — no new-component onboarding chains and no\n84\t  konflux-onboarding epics are needed; all are already Konflux-onboarded.\n85\t\n86\t## Investigation Analysis\n87\t\n88\tUnknowns scanned from Open Questions, risks, and assumptions:\n89\t\n90\t1. **Hardware access** (Open Question 1): procurement/partner-lab access for\n91\t   dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is unresolved. The answer does not change which\n92\t   epics exist or what they do — the campaign happens either way — so this is an\n93\t   availability precondition, not an Investigation. It is recorded as a risk on\n94\t   E001; securing access is part of E001's work, but access being granted is not\n95\t   an acceptance criterion (externally controlled).\n96\t2. **Upstream ROCm build cadence** (Open Question 2): whether AMD's runtime\n97\t   image releases align with release trains. This affects the image-sourcing\n98\t   approach inside E002 (consume AMD images vs self-build), not the existence or\n99\t   boundaries of any epic. Recorded by the campaign (which images it used) and\n100\t   handled as an AC + risk on E002.\n101\t3. **Parity outcomes per model** (HLR-1): the go/no-go and cleared-model list\n102\t   genuinely change what E002 does → Investigation epic E001 with a `rewrite`\n103\t   gate on E002.\n104\t\n105\t## Tiered Delivery\n106\t\n107\tThe external dependency (AMD hardware access and AMD-controlled ROCm image\n108\tcadence) has uncertain timing; partial value is deliverable without it:\n109\t\n110\t- **Tier 1** (independent, starts immediately, parallel): E001 campaign\n111\t  (risk-reduction validation), E003 training support, E004 Kueue quota\n112\t  handling.\n113\t- **Tier 2** (gated): E002 serving runtime productization, gated by the E001\n114\t  go/no-go.\n115\t- E005 documentation completes after all implementations (Rule 11).\n116\t\n117\t## Health Warnings\n118\t\n119\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n120\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n121\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from the architecture-context\n122\t  owning teams (RHOAI Serving: KServe, Serving Runtimes; Distributed Workloads:\n123\t  Training Operator, Distributed Workloads). Epic-level ACs were derived from\n124\t  HLR wording.\n125\t- **Infrastructure not in platform inventory**: HLR-3 assumes `amd.com/gpu`\n126\t  resources on nodes; the component that exposes them (AMD GPU operator / ROCm\n127\t  device plugin stack) is not in the platform inventory — the architecture\n128\t  context lists only the NVIDIA GPU Operator, as an external dependency rather\n129\t  than a platform component. The validation campaign (E001) includes standing\n130\t  up and validating this stack on the campaign nodes (Rule 22 validation). If\n131\t  the go/no-go clears, platform-level support for the AMD GPU operator on\n132\t  customer clusters is not covered by the strategy's Affected Components and\n133\t  needs a follow-up scope decision.\n134\t- **Docs team assumption**: E005 is assigned to RHOAI Serving by assumption\n135\t  (Documentation has no architecture-context file and the strategy names no\n136\t  docs owner). Confirm the owning team before scheduling.\n137\t- **E001 size**: E001 is estimated at ~2-3 sprints (cluster stack bring-up plus\n138\t  per-model functional and performance runs across the supported model set);\n139\t  kept whole because the go/no-go requires the full model-set picture from one\n140\t  consistent hardware/stack configuration, and a split would fragment the parity\n141\t  dataset across different node states. Re-split trigger: if the supported model\n142\t  set exceeds ~8 models or stack bring-up consumes more than one sprint, split\n143\t  the per-model-group parity runs into follow-on validation epics that reuse\n144\t  the stood-up cluster configuration.\n145\t- **No priority inversions detected**: E001 is P0 both by HLR-1 and by blocking\n146\t  the P0 productization epic; E003/E004 are P1 matching HLR-3; E005 is P1 by the\n147\t  docs-authoring exception (HLR-4 itself is P2 — the epic priority is derived\n148\t  from the strategy's overall `Major` priority, not pushed onto upstream epics).\n149\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0fc9c831c059812","agent_id":"aa48f3792034417b5","session_id":"1580befd-2500-423d-8dd5-ee8176ac89f3","uuid":"23362e13-262c-46a8-9643-2722685ac7b2","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:30:38.005Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf9438fe55d2c226","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
